If a mapping rule resolves to a datasource that isn’t registered, this code silently falls back to other drivers. That can route reads/writes to the wrong database without any signal. Consider throwing when `mappedDatasource` is returned but `this.drivers` doesn’t contain it (mirroring the explicit object.datasource behavior).
```suggestion
    if (mappedDatasource) {
      if (!this.drivers.has(mappedDatasource)) {
        throw new Error(
          `[ObjectQL] Datasource '${mappedDatasource}' resolved from mapping for object '${objectName}' is not registered.`
        );
      }
```

Sorting by `priority ?? 1000` changes the documented behavior “if not specified, rules are evaluated in array order”. With the current sort, any rule that sets a priority will jump ahead of earlier rules with no priority. Consider preserving original index as a tiebreaker and only applying priority ordering among rules that explicitly specify it (or document that any priority usage switches the whole list to priority-based evaluation).
```suggestion
    // Preserve declared rule order by default and only apply priority ordering
    // among rules that explicitly define a priority.
    const sortedRules = this.datasourceMapping
      .map((rule, index) => ({ rule, index }))
      .sort((a, b) => {
        const aHasPriority = a.rule.priority !== undefined;
        const bHasPriority = b.rule.priority !== undefined;

        if (aHasPriority && bHasPriority) {
          const priorityDiff = a.rule.priority - b.rule.priority;
          return priorityDiff !== 0 ? priorityDiff : a.index - b.index;
        }

        return a.index - b.index;
      })
      .map(({ rule }) => rule);
```
